venus: ignore a fence retirement for a context that is already gone

Applies to: slp/virglrenderer tag 0.10.4e-krunkit (bfec2d27c3953ee0530c459c2f5d042c2b619fa1).

venus_state_cb_retire_fence() is called from a Vulkan queue thread of the
renderer. A queue thread can retire a fence after the guest context was torn
down (the guest process exited or the context was destroyed while the GPU was
still busy). venus_lookup_context() then returns NULL and the assert() aborts
the whole VMM process (SIGSEGV with NDEBUG, SIGABRT otherwise). This was one of
the two crash types seen with timeline semaphores in the Venus spike (REPORT.md
B3).

Do what the surrounding code already does for stale callbacks: drop the
retirement when the context no longer exists.

This is a symptom fix: it does not make the teardown race itself go away (the
queue thread can still race the context destruction). It only turns a certain
crash into a no-op.

Upstream status: not applicable. src/venus_context.c (the in-process Venus
context) exists only in the macOS fork; upstream runs Venus in a separate render
server and has no such callback.

---
 src/venus_context.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/venus_context.c b/src/venus_context.c
index 33559c9..481c02b 100644
--- a/src/venus_context.c
+++ b/src/venus_context.c
@@ -422,7 +422,9 @@ static void
 venus_state_cb_retire_fence(uint32_t ctx_id, uint32_t ring_idx, uint64_t fence_id)
 {
    struct venus_context *ctx = venus_lookup_context(ctx_id);
-   assert(ctx);
+   /* A queue thread can retire a fence after the guest context was torn down. */
+   if (!ctx)
+      return;

    const uint32_t seqno = (uint32_t)fence_id;
    venus_context_retire_fences_internal(ctx, ring_idx, seqno);
